EXAMPLES=[
 {'id':'we-4-source-18','num':'18','title':'Example 18','problem':'Find tangent and normal lines to y = x² at P(2,4).','given':'A parabola and a point on it.','method':'Find the derivative slope and use point–slope form.','steps':['Check the point: 2² = 4, so (2,4) lies on the curve.','Differentiate: y′ = 2x. At x = 2, the tangent slope is 4.','The tangent equation is y − 4 = 4(x − 2).','Simplify to y = 4x − 4, or 4x − y − 4 = 0.','The perpendicular normal slope is −1/4. Its equation is y − 4 = −(x − 2)/4.','Multiply by 4 and collect to obtain x + 4y − 18 = 0.','Check both lines at (2,4): 8 − 4 − 4 = 0 and 2 + 16 − 18 = 0. Their slopes multiply to −1.'],'answer':'Tangent: 4x − y − 4 = 0. Normal: x + 4y − 18 = 0.','sourceReferences':[{'pageStart':115,'pageEnd':116,'verification':'source question and complete proof compared with JPG/PDF'}]},
 {'id':'we-4-source-19','num':'19','title':'Example 19','problem':'Find tangent and normal lines to y = 9 − x² where the curve crosses the x-axis.','given':'A downward parabola; y = 0 at an x-axis crossing.','method':'Find both crossings and differentiate for the two slopes.','steps':['Put y = 0: x² = 9, so the crossings are (3,0) and (−3,0).','The derivative is −2x. At x = 3 it is −6; at x = −3 it is 6.','At (3,0), the tangent is y = −6(x − 3), or 6x + y − 18 = 0.','At (−3,0), the tangent is y = 6(x + 3), or 6x − y + 18 = 0.','Normal slopes are 1/6 and −1/6 respectively.','At (3,0), y = (x − 3)/6, so x − 6y − 3 = 0.','At (−3,0), y = −(x + 3)/6, so x + 6y + 3 = 0.','Each pair passes through its crossing and has perpendicular slopes; keep the positive and negative crossings associated with their own lines.'],'answer':'At (3,0): tangent 6x + y − 18 = 0, normal x − 6y − 3 = 0. At (−3,0): tangent 6x − y + 18 = 0, normal x + 6y + 3 = 0.','sourceReferences':[{'page':116,'jpg':'062_page_2.jpg','verification':'source question and complete proof compared with JPG/PDF'}]},
 {'id':'we-4-source-20','num':'20','title':'Example 20','problem':'Find the angle between y = x³ − 2x + 1 and y = x² + 1 at (2,5).','given':'Two curves and the specified common point.','method':'Differentiate both curves and compare their tangent slopes.','steps':['Check the common point: 8 − 4 + 1 = 5 and 4 + 1 = 5.','For all intersections, subtract the curve equations: x³ − x² − 2x = x(x − 2)(x + 1) = 0.','The intersections are (0,1),(−1,2),(2,5). The question selects (2,5).','First slope: 3x² − 2 = 10 at x = 2. Second slope: 2x = 4.','The smaller angle has tangent |(10 − 4)/(1 + 10·4)| = 6/41.','Thus θ = arctan(6/41) ≈ 0.145310011 rad ≈ 8.3256503°.','The denominator is 41, so the tangents are not perpendicular; the source 0.1453 is a rounded radian value.'],'answer':'θ = arctan(6/41) ≈ 0.14531 radians, or 8.32565°.','sourceReferences':[{'pageStart':116,'pageEnd':117,'verification':'source question and complete proof compared with JPG/PDF'}]},
 {'id':'we-4-source-21','num':'21','title':'Example 21','problem':'Find all points on y = 2x³ + 4x² whose tangent is parallel to y = 8x − 4.','given':'The line’s slope is 8.','method':'Set the derivative equal to the line’s slope and evaluate the curve.','steps':['Differentiate: y′ = 6x² + 8x.','Parallel tangents need 6x² + 8x = 8, or 3x² + 4x − 4 = 0.','Factor: (x + 2)(3x − 2) = 0, so x = −2 or 2/3.','At x = −2, y = 2(−8) + 4(4) = 0.','At x = 2/3, y = 16/27 + 16/9 = 64/27.','Check both slopes: 6(4) − 16 = 8 and 6(4/9) + 8(2/3) = 8.','The requested points are (−2,0) and (2/3,64/27). Optional tangent equations are y = 8(x + 2) and y − 64/27 = 8(x − 2/3).'],'answer':'(−2,0) and (2/3,64/27).','sourceReferences':[{'page':117,'jpg':'063_page_1.jpg','verification':'source question and complete proof compared with JPG/PDF'}]}
]
